 { Welcome to the Positional Chess Patterns Manual! I'm delighted you've chosen to embark on this positional chess journey with me. In this introduction, I want to share what this course is about and how it came to be. As a chess coach for many years, primarily working with intermediate players rated between 1400 and 2000, I’ve observed a common trend: my students rarely suffer from a lack of dedication. They are in general very motivated to improve at the game that they love. Their problem, instead, is that they are often unclear on how or what they should study to make the leap to the next level! In fact, it’s often the frustration that comes with this lack of a clear training plan that motivates them to begin working with a coach in the first place. You see, without clear direction, most students settle into a routine that includes tactics puzzles, studying their favorite openings, and occasionally dipping into endgame theory. They will then build out their strategic understanding by reading a few classic books, like Mikhail Tal’s Life and Games or Bobby Fischer’s My 60 Memorable Games. While this routine isn’t inherently bad and can lead to gradual improvement, it often results in a plateau, where progress slows or feels stagnant. The root of this problem, in my view, lies in the nature of learning.. Humans learn best when fully engaged, and this applies to chess just as much as it does to any other skill, whether it's programming, playing a musical instrument, or swimming. In their normal routine, most students actively engage with their openings and tactics puzzles. However, when it comes to strategic topics like positional play, they are usually learning the concepts passively, much like you would if you were reading a novel. This passive approach makes it difficult to translate one’s understanding into practical decision making at the chessboard. As a result, students find themselves making the type of mistakes that they feel were avoidable. However, in spite of their best efforts, their play continues to be plagued by such mistakes in future battles. The issue, to my eyes, is not a lack of understanding. Instead, it is about the gap between learning a concept, and being able to apply it in competition. This course aims to bridge this gap by focusing not just on teaching positional concepts, but on giving you the opportunity to train the concepts after you learn them. From beginning to end, you will have the opportunity to solve ~300 positional puzzles, so that you will not only understand the concepts - but you will build up experience on how to spot and apply the concepts in real life situations. The course is designed for intermediate players, and is structured around eight major chapters. We’ll usually begin with the basics and build up in complexity as we delve deeper into each major topic. In my view, this form of first principles learning, where you break down processes into their simplest parts, is the most effective way to build a strong foundation and can be applied to not only positional chess, but to any part of chess learning. One more thing I wish to mention is on the topic of how the material was compiled. I looked through a little over 10,000 games, ranging from old-school games featuring great technicians of the past like Capablanca, as well as many games from the best player of modern times: Magnus Carlsen. The goal was to extract the most common types of mistakes and later to categorize them according to the various themes. In this way, my hope is that the material fairly represents the most typical situations and decisions that should be studied by the player who is seeking to take their positional play to the next level. As a quick summary, we can say that there were three main elements that influenced the creation and structure of this course: 1. A hybrid approach, involving both theory and puzzle sections. This allows for active learning and will put you in position to use the concepts learned next time that you find yourself in the heat of battle! 2. A first principles approach, meaning that we try to find what are the core elements that must be understood to develop into a strong positional player. We begin with simple examples, and later on use this knowledge to build up to more complicated concepts. In this way, you will build a sturdy foundation that will serve you well when tackling more advanced topics later on in your chess journey. 3. Focusing on common situations. By drawing from thousands of games, we can be confident that we will end up with a well-balanced knowledge of the most common situations and patterns that are important to spot at the chessboard. We’ll look at patterns in a broad sense, like outposts and weak squares. And we’ll also narrow our focus and dive into more specific patterns, like the 4 vs 3 pawn majority, or central pawn breaks. I hope that this introduction has helped to give you an idea of what’s to come, and I wish you all the best on the positional chess journey that you have in front of you! 🙂 Good luck! Alex Astaneh } 1. -- 
