 { Below you can find a quick summary of the material that lies ahead. I recommend that you work your way through the course in chronological order, as in subsequent chapters we will often use and build upon the topics introduced previously. (1) Weak Squares Weak squares are squares that cannot be controlled by an enemy pawn. This kind of weakness lies at the heart of a lot of positional strategies and so is the focus of our first chapter. We'll discuss the value of weak squares and how you can exploit them to gain an advantage. We’ll also speak about how to defend your own weak squares, as well as how to target an entire group of weak squares. (2) Outposts An outpost is like a weak square, but even stronger! This is because here the square is also protected by a friendly pawn. This makes them very valuable positional assets, and it's not uncommon to see experienced players battling for long stretches of a game over control of a single outpost. We'll discuss how to create outposts and how to fight for control of them. We'll also cover special types of outposts like power or pseudo outposts. More on these later! (3) Space Space refers to which side controls the most squares on the chessboard. The tricky part is that more space is not always a good thing! We'll talk about how to evaluate space, how you can push home a space advantage, as well as how you can fight against space when your opponent is trying to squeeze you to death! (4) Central Control The centre is a common battleground at the chessboard, as even beginner chess players soon learn of its great importance! First we’ll speak about the power of the centre, and later we'll discuss how you can use the centre to your advantage when you have it - and how you can fight against the centre when you don't! (5) Piece Play In this chapter we shift the focus to the pieces! We’ll cover piece activity and how you can take advantage of your opponent’s passive pieces. We’ll then speak about making the right piece exchanges, as well as typical dynamics between bishops and knights in the section on minor piece battles. We’ll end the chapter by exploring the dynamic world of positional sacrifices, including the popular exchange sacrifice. (6) Open Files In this chapter we’ll cover both open and semi-open files. We’ll discuss the most important positional themes relating to open files, like the value of occupying the seventh rank with one of your rooks, the fight for key entry squares; targeting backward pawns; and lastly: how to combine open files with outposts. (7) Pawn Play In this chapter we'll take a look at situations where the pawns are actively used to gain an advantage. We’ll do a deep dive on pawn breaks and later speak about how you can use your pawns to target the opponent’s pawn structure as well as their minor pieces. We’ll close out the chapter with coverage on the powerful 4 vs 3 pawn majority, and a selection of common pawn types like backward pawns, hanging pawns, and the IQP among others. (8) Endgame Play The final theory chapter will touch on four important themes. The first of these is schematic thinking, a form of thinking and planning that is essential to play strong chess in the endgame phase. Other topics that we’ll cover include piece paralysis and fortresses, while in the final section of the chapter we will turn to the one piece that we have largely ignored throughout the course: the king! (9) Final Test Throughout the course, you will have had the opportunity to work on themed puzzles at the end of each of the major chapters. Now, there is one final test that awaits and this contains 32 puzzles that are completely mixed and will serve to refresh and test the various concepts covered throughout the course. Happy solving! 🙂 } 1. -- 
