fxe4 $1 15. Bxe4 Nd5 $1 { White remains better and went on to win the game, though Black has kept the fight very much alive due to seizing control of the powerful outpost on d5. I hope that these couple of introductory examples have been a helpful illustration of the general approach that we will take in this course. The plan is going to be to first study the cornerstone topics of positional play in depth, chapter by chapter. As we build on this knowledge and test our skill via puzzles, we will get increasingly more confident at tying the various elements of positional play together. In time, the goal is to grow in confidence and be able to execute complete positional strategies even under the pressure of competitive chess play! I wish you the best of luck working your way through the material in this course, and I hope that it meets your goals and expectations as you take on this new challenge in your chess journey. } 
